This ensures you’re using Turbopack for normal next dev runs.

🧪 2️⃣ Use a Custom Script for Tests (Webpack + Babel)

Since Turbopack doesn’t support Babel plugins, you’ll need to temporarily switch to Webpack during Cypress tests.

Add a separate test script in your package.json:

"scripts": {
  "dev": "next dev",
  "build": "next build",
  "start": "next start",
  "test:coverage": "NEXT_TEST_MODE=true next dev"
}


Then modify your next.config.js:

const nextConfig = {
  experimental: {
    turbo: process.env.NEXT_TEST_MODE ? false : true,
  },
  webpack(config, { dev, isServer }) {
    if (process.env.NEXT_TEST_MODE) {
      config.module.rules.push({
        test: /\.(js|ts|jsx|tsx)$/,
        exclude: /node_modules/,
        use: {
          loader: 'babel-loader',
          options: {
            plugins: [
              'istanbul' // instrument code for coverage
            ],
          },
        },
      });
    }
    return config;
  },
};

module.exports = nextConfig;


✅ Now, when you run:

npm run test:coverage


it’ll disable Turbopack, enable Webpack + Babel, and instrument your code for Cypress coverage — without touching your normal dev setup.

📁 3️⃣ Add a .babelrc for Test Mode

You can keep a .babelrc like this:

{
  "env": {
    "test": {
      "plugins": ["istanbul"]
    }
  }
}


This ensures Babel only runs when NODE_ENV=test.

🧾 4️⃣ Configure Cypress Code Coverage

Your cypress.config.ts already looks good.
Just make sure to add the coverage support file:

