💡 Excellent and very fair DevOps perspective — you’ve highlighted some of the exact friction points that come up when running Next.js in containerized environments. Let me try to summarize some best practices and possible directions:

🧩 1. Runtime Configuration

You’re absolutely right — NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables are baked at build time, which breaks immutable build workflows.

The pattern we use in CI/CD (especially with Kubernetes) is to load runtime variables from a mounted JSON or JS file instead of environment variables:

Example:

// public/runtime-config.js
window.RUNTIME_CONFIG = {
  API_URL: "%%API_URL%%",
  APP_ENV: "%%APP_ENV%%"
};


Then, replace placeholders (%%API_URL%%) at container startup using envsubst or sed.
On the frontend, read them like:

const apiUrl = window.RUNTIME_CONFIG?.API_URL;


✅ This avoids rebuilds, preserves immutable images, and supports runtime injection — similar to how SPA frameworks handle runtime configs.

🧠 2. Next.js as a “Frontend-only” Deployment

For apps that don’t need SSR, you can use:

next build && next export


This generates a fully static version under /out, which can be served via nginx, Netlify, or CloudFront without Node.js.

You lose SSR and API routes, but the build becomes extremely lightweight — perfect for traditional frontend hosting or microfrontend-style setups.

🪵 3. Logging Clarity

The “fetch failed” messages you’re seeing often come from undici (Node’s internal fetch).
You can improve this by enabling custom request logging:

globalThis.fetch = async (...args) => {
  try {
    return await import('node-fetch').then(({ default: fetch }) => fetch(...args));
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("[fetch-error]", args[0], err.message);
    throw err;
  }
};


Additionally, when deploying with Vercel or Docker, set:

NODE_OPTIONS="--enable-source-maps"


This keeps stack traces readable in production.

🚀 Final Thoughts

You’re not missing anything — Next.js does blur the lines between backend and frontend.
But using runtime config injection + static exports + custom logging can make it behave much more DevOps-friendly without giving up its modern tooling benefits.

References

Next.js Static Export

[Runtime Configuration Strategies](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/37179)

Node.js Source Maps for Production

Hope this helps clarify some deployment pain points — your post will definitely help make Next.js more accessible to DevOps teams 👏

