Hey @ramusesan,

You're seeing the whole AEM page disappear because the Edge Worker is returning the full Next.js HTML document (with `<html><body>…`) and replacing the parent page.

Two clean ways:

1. **Just use an iframe** (fastest)
   ```html
   <iframe src="https://xyz.com/your-page" style="width:100%;height:800px;border:none;"></iframe>
   ```
   Add this to `next.config.js` so it allows framing:
   ```js
   async headers() {
     return [
       {
         source: "/:path*",
         headers: [
           { key: "X-Frame-Options", value: "ALLOWALL" },
           { key: "Content-Security-Policy", value: "frame-ancestors *;" }
         ]
       }
     ]
   }
   ```

2. **Widget mode (no iframe)**
   Make the page render only the content when embedded:
   ```tsx
   // app/widget-page/page.tsx or layout.tsx
   const isEmbedded = typeof window !== "undefined" && window.top !== window;

   if (isEmbedded) return <>{children}</>;

   return (
     <html><body>{children}</body></html>
   );
   ```
   Then load it with a simple script tag in AEM/Angular instead of proxying the whole HTML.

Iframe is the quickest fix. Try that first.

Hey @ramusesan, got it – you're injecting the raw HTML from your Next.js app into a `<div>` inside AEM, then Next.js hydrates and blows away the rest of the page because it thinks it's the only thing on the page. Classic hydration mismatch when you drop a full React/Next root into someone else's DOM.

Here’s the proper widget/embedded mode that actually works in Next 15 (app router):

1. Create a dedicated embed layout (or just a page if you only have one)

```tsx
// app/embed/[[...slug]]/layout.tsx   (catch-all so any route works)
import { ReactNode } from 'react'

export default function EmbedLayout({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  // If we're inside an iframe or injected into another page, skip <html>/<body>
  const isEmbedded = typeof window !== 'undefined' && window.top !== window.self

  if (isEmbedded) {
    return <>{children}</>
  }

  // Normal full page when visited directly
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  )
}
```

2. Put whatever page/component you want to embed under that route

```tsx
// app/embed/dashboard/page.tsx
export default function Dashboard() {
  return <div className="p-8 bg-gray-100">Your widget content here</div>
}
```

3. Deploy, then in AEM just do:

```html
<div id="nextjs-widget"></div>

<script 
  dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{
    __html: `
      fetch('https://xyz.com/embed/dashboard')
        .then(r => r.text())
        .then(html => {
          document.getElementById('nextjs-widget').innerHTML = html;
        });
    `
  }}>
</script>
```

Next.js will see it's embedded, skip rendering `<html><body>`, hydrate only inside your div, and everything coexists peacefully with AEM/Angular.

Bonus: Cookie sharing  
You still can't read httpOnly cookies cross-origin, but if you make your API routes same-site (or use a shared subdomain like `app.xyz.com` and `widget.xyz.com` with `SameSite=Lax` + `Domain=.xyz.com`), cookies will be sent automatically on fetch/axios calls from the widget.

Iframe works too if you just add `allow="cookies"` and relax the CSP, but the method above is cleaner and faster.

