Anyway, We’ve figured out a few approaches that work pretty well for us.

First thing I do is I always do optimistic redirects in the proxy (formerly middleware). I check for a cookie there, and if it doesn’t exist, I redirect to login. (I don’t fetch there for the real session, because fetching in the proxy will slow down your whole app.) Then, if the proxy still detects a cookie and lets the request through, we need to check for the real session in the DB. This is where I use a data access layer (not sure if that’s the right term in this case, but that’s what we call it).

First, we have a cached getSession function:

```
import { headers } from "next/headers";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";
import { cache } from "react";
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";

export const getSession = cache(async () => {
  const session = await auth.api.getSession({ headers: await headers() });

  // NOTE: This is only reached if the proxy does not handle the redirect.
  // In this case, we need to redirect to an endpoint that will remove the cookie.
  if (!session) redirect("/api/auth/corrupted-cookie");

  return session;
});
```

(We use Better Auth, if anyone is wondering.)

Then there are two patterns for how we use it:

1) Access protected data in RSC
```
import type { FC } from "react";
import { getSession } from "@/lib/auth";

const UserName = async () => {
  const session = await getSession();
  return <div>User Name: {session.user.name}</div>;
};

async function listUserPosts() {
  const session = await getSession();
  return await db.queryPosts(session.user.id);
}

const UserPosts = async () => {
  const posts = await listUserPosts();
  return posts.map((post) => <div key={post.id}>{post.title}</div>);
};

const Page: FC<PageProps<"/">> = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <Suspense>
         <UserName />
      </Suspense> 
      <Suspense>
         <UserPosts />
      </Suspense>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Page;
```

2) Access data in the client (or multiple places in the client)
We create a promise at the top level and use it wherever we need in the client:
```
import type { FC } from "react";
import { getSession } from "@/lib/auth";

async function listUserPosts() {
  const session = await getSession();
  return await db.queryPosts(session.user.id);
}

const Page: FC<PageProps<"/">> = () => {
  const postsPromise = listUserPosts();

  return (
    <div>
      <Suspense>
         <PostsCount postsPromise={postsPromise} />
      </Suspense>
      <Suspense>
         <PostsList postsPromise={postsPromise} />
      </Suspense>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Page;

// Client
"use client";

import { use } from "react";

const PostsCount = ({ postsPromise }) => {
  const posts = use(postsPromise);
  return <div>Posts Count: {posts.length}</div>;
};

const PostsList = ({ postsPromise }) => {
  const posts = use(postsPromise);
  return posts.map((post) => <div key={post.id}>{post.title}</div>);
};
```

Not sure if this is the best way to do it, but it works for us and we get all the benefits of PPR. However, it really takes time to get used to. Also these examples are top of my head and I believe can differ strongly based on your use case.

