Hey, You are spot on about the Activity component behavior. This is a classic "State vs. Props" synchronization challenge.

Because the Activity component keeps the Client Component instance alive, the `useState(initialTodos)` initializer never runs again when you navigate back, leaving you with stale data.

While `useEffect` solves this, it effectively causes a "double render" (Mount → Paint Stale → Effect → State Update → Re-render → Paint Fresh).

### The Cleaner Alternative

Instead of syncing state manually, you can force React to treat the component as "new" when the data changes by using the `key` prop in the parent Server Component.

```tsx
// todos/page.tsx
export default async function Page() {
  const todosPromise = getTodos();
  
  // Use a unique value here to signal "new data"
  // This could be a timestamp, a request ID, or a hash of the params
  const renderKey = new Date().getTime(); 

  return (
    <Suspense>
      {/* Changing the key forces React to dump the old state and re-initialize */}
      <ClientComponent 
          todosPromise={todosPromise} 
          key={renderKey} 
      />
    </Suspense>
  );
}

