Hey, you are hitting a hard architectural constraint of the Edge runtime, but the "overhead" concern is actually solvable with a better matcher regex.

**The Constraint:**
The `matcher` **must** be static because it compiles into the routing rules at the CDN/Infrastructure level (e.g., Vercel's Edge Network) *before* any JavaScript is executed. The router needs to know whether to even spin up the Middleware sandbox without running your code first.

**The Workaround:**
You cannot change the `matcher` dynamically, but you **can** optimize it to exclude static assets (images, fonts, Next.js chunks). This eliminates 90% of the "unnecessary overhead" you are worried about.

For the remaining requests, an "Early Return" strategy inside Middleware takes microseconds on the Edge and is the standard pattern for Maintenance Mode.

Here is the optimized pattern:

```ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'
import type { NextRequest } from 'next/server'

export const config = {
  // 1. NEGATIVE LOOKAHEAD MATCHER
  // This matches ALL routes except static files, images, and internal Next.js chunks.
  // This effectively removes the overhead for assets, which is the expensive part.
  matcher: [
    '/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico|.*\\.(?:svg|png|jpg|jpeg|gif|webp)$).*)',
  ],
}

export function middleware(req: NextRequest) {
  const isMaintenanceMode = process.env.MAINTENANCE_MODE === "true";
  const { pathname } = req.nextUrl;

  // --- SCENARIO 1: Maintenance Mode is ON ---
  if (isMaintenanceMode) {
    if (pathname === "/under-maintenance") return NextResponse.next();
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL("/under-maintenance", req.url));
  }

  // --- SCENARIO 2: Maintenance Mode is OFF ---
  // We matched everything to support Scenario 1, but now we want to exit FAST.
  
  const targetedPaths = ["/login", "/verify-otp", "/signup", "/user"];
  const isTargeted = targetedPaths.some(path => pathname.startsWith(path));

  // If this request isn't for our targeted paths, exit immediately.
  // The cost of this check on the Edge is negligible (microseconds).
  if (!isTargeted) {
    return NextResponse.next();
  }

  // ... Run your Proxy/Auth logic here ...
  return NextResponse.next();
}
```

**Why this works:**
1.  The `matcher` regex prevents the Middleware from waking up for images and JS files (saving cost/latency).
2.  The `if (!isTargeted) return next()` handles the "dynamic" logic. Since Middleware runs on the Edge, this boolean check is effectively instantaneous and doesn't add measurable latency to your public pages.

Hey, This is a classic **Hydration Mismatch**.

The issue happens inside your `useState` initialization in `RightPanelProvider`.
On the **Server**, `document` is undefined, so `getCookie` returns `null`. React renders the panel as **Closed/Empty**.
On the **Client**, `document.cookie` exists, so React initializes the state as **Open/Chat**.

Because the Server HTML and Client HTML don't match, React throws a hydration error and discards the server HTML, causing the flash/delay you are seeing.

**The Fix:**
You need to read the cookies in your **Server Component** (`RootLayout`) and pass them as props to the provider. This ensures both Server and Client start with the exact same data.

### 1. Update `RightPanelProvider` to accept initial view
Modify your provider to accept a `defaultPanelView` prop and use it in `useState`.

```tsx
// components/ui/right-panel.tsx

// 1. Add defaultPanelView to the props type
export function RightPanelProvider({
  defaultOpen = false,
  defaultPanelView = null, // Add this prop
  open: openProp,
  onOpenChange: setOpenProp,
  // ... other props
}: React.ComponentProps<"div"> & {
  defaultOpen?: boolean;
  defaultPanelView?: PanelView; // Add type definition
  open?: boolean;
  onOpenChange?: (open: boolean) => void;
}) {
  
  // ... existing code ...

  // 2. Simplify internal state to use the props directly
  // We don't need to read cookies here anymore because the prop comes from the server
  const [_open, _setOpen] = React.useState(defaultOpen);

  // 3. Update panelView state to use the prop
  const [panelView, setPanelViewState] = React.useState<PanelView>(defaultPanelView);

  // ... rest of the component
```

### 2. Update `RootLayout` to read cookies
Change your layout to an `async` function, read the cookies using `next/headers`, and pass them down.

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
import { cookies } from "next/headers"; // Import cookies

export default async function RootLayout({ // Make this async
  children,
}: Readonly<{
  children: React.ReactNode;
}>) {
  // Read cookies on the server
  const cookieStore = await cookies();
  const defaultOpen = cookieStore.get("right_panel_state")?.value === "true";
  const defaultView = cookieStore.get("right_panel_view")?.value || null;

  return (
    <SidebarProvider>
      {/* Pass the server-read values as props */}
      <RightPanelProvider 
        defaultOpen={defaultOpen} 
        defaultPanelView={defaultView as any}
      >
        <AppSidebar />
        <SidebarInset className="...">
          {children}
        </SidebarInset>

        <RightPanel>
          <Suspense fallback={"Loading..."}>
            <RightPanelRouter />
          </Suspense>
        </RightPanel>
      </RightPanelProvider>
    </SidebarProvider>
  );
}
```

**Why this works:**
Using `cookies()` in the layout ensures that the initial HTML sent from the server already has the panel "Open" and set to "Chat". When the browser takes over, the props match the HTML, eliminating the hydration error and the delay.

