This appears to be a Turbopack + next/font/local interaction issue that causes the build to fail when resolving static assets, eventually throwing:

```
StaticAsset::path: not found
Failed to write app endpoint /page
```

**Workaround**

Instead of using:

```
import localFont from "next/font/local";
```


I manually defined the fonts using @font-face in globals.css, for example:

```
@font-face {
  font-family: "MyFont";
  src: url("/fonts/MyFont-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-weight: 400;
  font-style: normal;
  font-display: swap;
}

```

Then referenced the font normally via CSS variables / font-family.

**Result**

- Turbopack dev errors stopped occurring
- vercel build and production deployment completed successfully
- No regression in font loading behavior

This is a viable workaround until the underlying next/font/local + Turbopack asset resolution issue is addressed.

CSS localization (RTL/LTR support) in Next.js App Router can be handled a few ways:

Option 1 — Set dir attribute on <html> dynamically (recommended):
In your app/[lang]/layout.tsx, set the direction based on the locale:


export default function Layout({ children, params: { lang } }) {
  const dir = ['ar', 'he', 'fa', 'ur'].includes(lang) ? 'rtl' : 'ltr';
  return (
    <html lang={lang} dir={dir}>
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
This makes the browser automatically flip text direction, and CSS logical properties (margin-inline-start, padding-inline-end, etc.) will respond accordingly.

Option 2 — Use CSS logical properties instead of left/right:
Replace physical properties with logical ones in your CSS:

/* Instead of: */
margin-left: 1rem;
/* Use: */
margin-inline-start: 1rem;
These flip automatically based on the dir attribute.

Option 3 — Separate RTL stylesheet:
Load a separate CSS file for RTL locales using a > tag conditionally in your layout.

The dir on <html> + CSS logical properties approach is the cleanest and most maintainable. Hope this helps!

Hi @sakib412 

## Why they can’t do it inside page.tsx (App Router)

In App Router, pages are streamed. That means headers must be decided before the React Server Component body starts streaming, so there is no supported equivalent to getServerSideProps({ res }) that can “wait for DB, then set headers, then render”. This is discussed by Next.js maintainers.

So the “official-ish” places to set headers are before render:

- next.config.js headers (static)

- Middleware / proxy (dynamic, but outside RSC render)

- A custom Node server (self-host only)

## Custom Node server that sets headers before Next renders

This is the closest replacement for getServerSideProps({ res }).

server.ts (Express + Next)
```ts
import express from "express";
import next from "next";

const dev = process.env.NODE_ENV !== "production";
const app = next({ dev });
const handle = app.getRequestHandler();

// Example: replace with your DB/ORM call
async function getPageHeaderConfig(pathname: string) {
  // e.g. map pathname -> slug -> DB lookup
  // return null if not found
  return {
    xFrameOptions: "DENY",
    csp: "default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none';",
    cacheControl: "private, no-store",
  };
}

function applyHeaders(res: express.Response, cfg: any) {
  if (!cfg) return;

  if (cfg.xFrameOptions) res.setHeader("X-Frame-Options", cfg.xFrameOptions);
  if (cfg.csp) res.setHeader("Content-Security-Policy", cfg.csp);
  if (cfg.cacheControl) res.setHeader("Cache-Control", cfg.cacheControl);

  // Add any other headers here:
  // res.setHeader("X-Robots-Tag", "noindex");
  // res.setHeader("Permissions-Policy", "geolocation=()");
}

app.prepare().then(() => {
  const server = express();

  server.all("*", async (req, res) => {
    try {
      const cfg = await getPageHeaderConfig(req.path);
      applyHeaders(res, cfg);

      // Then hand over to Next.js rendering
      return handle(req, res);
    } catch (e) {
      console.error(e);
      res.status(500).send("Server error");
    }
  });

  server.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log("Listening on http://localhost:3000");
  });
});
```
- True per-page dynamic headers

- No middleware limitations

- Works great when self-hosting Node

Notes : If you need CSP nonces, you’d generate a per-request nonce here, set it in CSP header, and pass it into the app (e.g., via a request header or cookie). Next’s CSP guide focuses on middleware, but the idea (nonce + CSP) is the same.


Other way :  Middleware sets headers (Edge) + shared cache

This is the common “Next.js way” for dynamic headers, but middleware runs outside the RSC render context.

middleware.ts
```ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from "next/server";

async function getHeaderConfig(pathname: string) {
  // Must be Edge-compatible (no Node-only libs)
  // Usually you call an internal API or a KV/Redis over HTTP.
  const res = await fetch(`${process.env.HEADERS_API}/headers?path=${encodeURIComponent(pathname)}`, {
    cache: "no-store",
  });

  if (!res.ok) return null;
  return res.json() as Promise<{
    xFrameOptions?: string;
    csp?: string;
    cacheControl?: string;
  }>;
}

export async function middleware(req: NextRequest) {
  const cfg = await getHeaderConfig(req.nextUrl.pathname);

  const res = NextResponse.next();

  if (cfg?.xFrameOptions) res.headers.set("X-Frame-Options", cfg.xFrameOptions);
  if (cfg?.csp) res.headers.set("Content-Security-Policy", cfg.csp);
  if (cfg?.cacheControl) res.headers.set("Cache-Control", cfg.cacheControl);

  return res;
}

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)"],
};
```
- Works on Vercel-style deployments

- Matches Next’s common security-header approach

- Next docs explicitly say headers must be set by returning a Response in route handlers/middleware style

@sakib412 there's no direct equivalent to `getServerSideProps({ res })` in App Router. [`headers()`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/headers) is read-only, and [`after()`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/after) (stable since 15.1.0) runs after the response is sent.

the pattern that solves both your problems (setting headers + avoiding duplicate API calls) is **middleware + request header forwarding**:

```ts
// middleware.ts
export async function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
  const config = await getPageConfig(request.nextUrl.pathname)

  const response = NextResponse.next({
    request: {
      headers: new Headers({
        ...Object.fromEntries(request.headers),
        'x-page-config': JSON.stringify(config),
      }),
    },
  })

  if (config?.xFrameOptions) response.headers.set('X-Frame-Options', config.xFrameOptions)
  if (config?.csp) response.headers.set('Content-Security-Policy', config.csp)
  return response
}
```

```ts
// app/[...slug]/page.tsx
export default async function Page() {
  const h = await headers()
  const config = JSON.parse(h.get('x-page-config') || '{}')
  return <PageBuilder config={config} />
}
```

middleware fetches the config once and passes it to the server component via a request header. no duplicate call. [`React.cache()`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/caching) only works inside the react tree, so middleware can't share its cache with RSC, but request headers bridge that gap.

if config size is a concern (risk of [431 errors](https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/HTTP/Status/431)), pass just the header values + a cache key, and let the RSC fetch the full config using `React.cache()`.

@sakib412 totally fair, 100kb+ builder json in headers is a nonstarter. your custom server + `AsyncLocalStorage` approach is the right call here.

the pattern is straightforward:

```ts
import { AsyncLocalStorage } from 'node:async_hooks'

const pageDataStore = new AsyncLocalStorage<BuilderData>()

// in your custom server handler:
const data = await fetchBuilderData(req.url)
pageDataStore.run(data, () => {
  // next.js handles the request inside this context
  nextHandler(req, res)
})

// in any server component / layout / page:
export function getPageData() {
  return pageDataStore.getStore()
}
```

the data never touches the client, no bandwidth doubling, and you can set response headers from the custom server before streaming starts. you do lose [automatic static optimization](https://nextjs.org/docs/pages/building-your-application/rendering/automatic-static-optimization) and edge runtime, but for a builder-driven site with per-request data that's the right tradeoff.

one heads-up: make sure you call `pageDataStore.run()` in the same async context that handles the request. if you use a reverse proxy or cluster, each worker needs its own store instance (they're process-scoped, not thread-safe across workers).

Hey @corysimmons — yeah, this is a known pain point. You've already found the right workaround, but let me add some context on *why* this happens and a couple of alternatives.

## Why the manifest ends up in `<body>`

When you use `manifest.ts` (or the `manifest` field in `metadata`), Next.js generates the `<link rel="manifest">` tag as part of the **metadata resolution** process. With RSC and streaming, metadata is resolved asynchronously and injected via the RSC payload — which means it gets streamed into the document *after* the initial HTML shell.

The initial shell contains the `<head>` with whatever static content is there, and then the body starts streaming. By the time the metadata resolves and the `<link rel="manifest">` is injected, the browser's parser has already moved past `<head>` into `<body>`. The browser doesn't move it back up — it just stays wherever it lands in the DOM.

Chrome (and other browsers) specifically look for `<link rel="manifest">` inside `<head>` per the [Web App Manifest spec](https://www.w3.org/TR/appmanifest/#obtaining). If it's in `<body>`, it's technically non-conformant and some browsers just ignore it.

## Your workaround is correct

What you're doing is the right fix:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <head>
        <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.json" />
      </head>
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

By placing the `<link>` directly in the `<head>` of your root layout, it's part of the **static shell** that gets sent immediately — before any streaming happens. Chrome will always see it.

## Alternative: use `manifest.webmanifest` in `/public`

If you want to keep things simple and avoid `manifest.ts` altogether:

1. Put your `manifest.webmanifest` (or `manifest.json`) in the `/public` directory
2. Reference it directly in your root layout's `<head>` as shown above

This way you don't rely on Next.js metadata resolution at all for the manifest.

## If you still want dynamic manifests

If your manifest needs to be dynamic (e.g., different `start_url` per locale), you can create a Route Handler:

```ts
// app/manifest.json/route.ts
export async function GET() {
  const manifest = {
    name: 'My App',
    short_name: 'App',
    start_url: '/',
    display: 'standalone',
    // ... other fields
  };

  return new Response(JSON.stringify(manifest), {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/manifest+json' },
  });
}
```

And still reference it with a static `<link>` in the root layout `<head>`.

## Related issues

This is closely related to the general problem of streamed metadata not being in `<head>` for initial HTML parse. There have been discussions about it:
- [#57087](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/57087) — metadata not in initial HTML
- [#64961](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/pull/64961) — improvements to metadata streaming

Hopefully the Next.js team will address this more holistically, but for now the direct `<link>` in `<head>` is the reliable solution.

Hope this clears things up!

## Expected Behavior for Invalid Dynamic Route URLs

When a user hits an invalid URL for a dynamic route (e.g., `/users/[id]` with an ID that doesn't exist in your database), Next.js does **not** automatically return a 404. The framework passes the parameter to your page, and **you are responsible for validation**.

## How to Handle Invalid Dynamic Parameters

### Option 1: Use `notFound()` in Server Components (Recommended)
```tsx
// app/users/[id]/page.tsx
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation';

async function getUser(id: string) {
  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) return null;
  return res.json();
}

export default async function UserPage({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  const user = await getUser(params.id);
  
  if (!user) {
    notFound(); // This triggers the not-found.tsx page
  }
  
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
}
```

### Option 2: Create a Custom `not-found.tsx`
```tsx
// app/users/[id]/not-found.tsx
export default function NotFound() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>User Not Found</h2>
      <p>The requested user does not exist.</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Option 3: Validate with `generateStaticParams` + `dynamicParams`

If you want to **restrict** dynamic routes to only pre-known values:
```tsx
// app/users/[id]/page.tsx
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const users = await fetch('https://api.example.com/users').then(r => r.json());
  return users.map((user) => ({ id: user.id.toString() }));
}

// Set this to false to return 404 for any ID not in generateStaticParams
export const dynamicParams = false;
```

With `dynamicParams = false`, any URL with an ID not returned by `generateStaticParams` will automatically 404.

## Summary

| Scenario | Behavior |
|----------|----------|
| No validation | Page renders with invalid param |
| Using `notFound()` | Shows `not-found.tsx` with 404 status |
| `dynamicParams = false` | Auto-404 for unlisted params |

## Docs Reference

- [Dynamic Routes](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/routing/dynamic-routes)
- [notFound function](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/not-found)
- [generateStaticParams](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/generate-static-params)

Thanks. I spent some time digging into this and there's actually quite a bit going on here. This is a good teaching moment ~~ for everyone

## You Might Not Need useEffect

The pattern in the original code is:

```jsx
const [selected, setSelected] = useState(initialValue);

useEffect(() => {
  if (pathName !== selected) {
    push(selected);
  }
}, [pathName, selected, pathSegments, push]);

// onChange:
onChange={() => setSelected(el.value)}
```

This is `onChange → setState → effect watches state → push`. But this should really just be:

```jsx
onChange={() => push(el.value)}
```

You might still want to track `selected` state for other purposes - but not to trigger the route change. The general principle: **effects are for synchronization with external systems, not for enacting state changes**. Router `push` is a state change (it's integrated into React's state via the router), not a sync operation.

This falls under the ["You Might Not Need an Effect"](https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect) guidance from the React docs.

## Some surprises along the way

The interesting part is that this code appears to work without `cacheComponents`. It was actually held together by several coincidences:

1. **React Compiler memoizing unstable deps** - `pathSegments` is recreated every render, which should cause the effect to fire constantly. The compiler memoizes it away. If you turn off the compiler, your effect immediately goes into run-away.

2. **Relative push semantics** - `push("slug-1")` is a relative navigation. From `/variant-one/slug-1`, it navigates to... `/variant-one/slug-1`. Same place.

3. **Router deduplication** - The router bails out when you push to the same location. So the infinite "push to where I already am" becomes a no-op.

4. **The comparison was always wrong** - `pathName !== selected` compares `/variant-one/slug-1` to `slug-1`. Always true. But because of `#2` and `#3`, it didn't matter. Though I spend a bit of time starting my debugging session from herer.

## Activity Exposed the Bugs

`cacheComponents`/Activity didn't cause these bugs - it makes them visible.

With Activity, you have two cached component instances in the DOM simultaneously. Each has its own React state, but `usePathname()` updates in both (it's an external subscription). When one is revealed:

- It has stale `selected` state from before it was hidden
- `pathName` is fresh from the router
- Mismatch triggers push
- Push reveals the other instance with its own stale state
- That one pushes back
- Ping-pong loop

There's also a form control issue: both instances have `<input name="commercial-property-type">`. Per HTML spec, radio buttons with the same `name` that **_aren't bound by a `<form>`_** are all part of the same group across the document. Two instances = four radios in one group, with conflicting checked states. One would need a unique `name` per instance-of-two-radio-buttons (for example, but not limited to, `useId()` or wrapping in separate `<form>` elements).

## a11y shows the way forward

While debugging this, I noticed the tab/arrow-key navigation causes focus loss - you navigate away and the focused element is gone. This happens with or without Activity.

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b629f0e5-738f-4ddd-ba65-cd73d596231e

> I tab into the radio group, and with arrow keys navigate to the next item. Focus is lost, because we swap the DOM from under the user. Try proper radio button focus here, https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/input/radio#try_it 

This suggests the component might belong in a **shared layout** rather than being duplicated per route. If it controls cross-route navigation, having one instance in the layout means:
- No caching/state issues
- No form control conflicts
- No focus loss on navigation

## Fixing it all up

### Step 1: Use Event Handlers, Not Effects

First things first - fix the pattern. No state for navigation, derive from URL, push in the handler:

```jsx
const { push } = useRouter();
const pathName = usePathname();
const id = useId();

const pathSegments = pathName.split("/").filter((segment) => segment);
const currentSlug = pathSegments[pathSegments.length - 1];

return (
  <form>
    {ROUTES.map((el) => (
      <label key={el.value} htmlFor={`${id}-${el.value}`}>
        <input
          type="radio"
          name="commercial-property-type"
          id={`${id}-${el.value}`}
          checked={currentSlug === el.value}
          onChange={() => push(el.value)}
        />
        {el.name}
      </label>
    ))}
  </form>
);
```

> Note: `name` doesn't need a prefix if wrapped in a `<form>` (scoped to form), but `id`/`htmlFor` still need `useId()` (document-global).

This fixes the infinite loop. But we're not done yet.

### Step 2: A11y - You Can't Lose Focus Like That

With the fix above, tab/arrow-key navigation still causes focus loss - you navigate away and the focused element is gone. That's not great for accessibility.

If a component controls cross-route navigation, it shouldn't be duplicated per route. Shared layout to the rescue.

**Layout:**
```jsx
// app/test-1/layout.tsx
import { Suspense } from "react";
import { SharedRadioGroup } from "@/components/SharedRadioGroup";

export default function Test1Layout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <main>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
        <SharedRadioGroup />
      </Suspense>
      {children}
    </main>
  );
}
```

**Component:**
```jsx
// components/SharedRadioGroup.tsx
"use client";

import { usePathname, useRouter } from "next/navigation";

export const SharedRadioGroup = () => {
  const { push } = useRouter();
  const pathName = usePathname();

  const pathSegments = pathName.split("/").filter((segment) => segment);
  const currentSlug = pathSegments[pathSegments.length - 1];

  return (
    <form>
      {ROUTES.map((el) => (
        <label key={el.value} htmlFor={el.value}>
          <input
            type="radio"
            name="commercial-property-type"
            id={el.value}
            value={el.value}
            checked={currentSlug === el.value}
            onChange={() => push(el.value)}
          />
          {el.name}
        </label>
      ))}
    </form>
  );
};
```

With a shared layout:
- **One instance** - no caching conflicts, no stale state
- **No unique instance names (`useId()`) needed** - single instance means the radio group has always 2 elements
- **Focus preserved** - radio group stays mounted across route changes
- **Proper a11y** - tab/arrow navigation works as expected


https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/fcf00394-5f5a-4491-83e1-801a7405a982

## TL;DR

1. Favor event handlers over effects for user-initiated actions
2. The original code had bugs masked by React Compiler + router deduplication
3. Activity exposed stale state issues with cached instances
4. Form controls need unique `name`/`id` when multiple instances exist in DOM
5. If the component controls cross-route navigation, it belongs in a shared layout

> And yes, I am sure there's some reason why the useEffect was needed, or whatever, business level code and teaching material, may have a gap sometimes.

Based on the research, here are the **current solutions** for setting response headers in App Router:

---

## ✅ Working Solutions

### 1. **Use Route Handlers (API Routes)**
```tsx
// app/api/product/[id]/route.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server';

export async function GET(request: Request, { params }) {
  const product = await getProduct(params.id);
  
  return NextResponse.json(product, {
    headers: {
      'Surrogate-Key': product.id,
      'Cache-Control': `s-maxage=${product.cacheTime}`,
    },
  });
}
```

### 2. **Use Middleware** (when headers don't depend on fetched data)
```tsx
// middleware.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server';

export function middleware(request) {
  const response = NextResponse.next();
  response.headers.set('X-Custom-Header', 'value');
  return response;
}
```

### 3. **Use `next.config.js`** (for static headers)
```js
// next.config.js
module.exports = {
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/products/:id',
        headers: [{ key: 'Cache-Control', value: 's-maxage=3600' }],
      },
    ];
  },
};
```

---

## ❌ Not Possible (by design)
Setting headers **inside Server Components** based on fetched data — because React streaming may have already started sending the response body.

---

## 🎯 Best Workaround for Dynamic Headers
Fetch data in a **Route Handler**, set headers there, then either:
- Return JSON and fetch client-side, OR
- Use the Route Handler as a proxy/redirect

This is the architectural limitation of streaming SSR.

