The existing reply covers the "why" well — CSS/SCSS imports have to be statically analyzable at build time, so template literals in import paths won't work. Turbopack is especially strict about this.

Here are a few approaches that actually work for multi-tenant theming in Next.js 16 App Router, depending on your needs:

## Option 1: CSS Custom Properties + data attributes (recommended)

The cleanest approach. One stylesheet, tenant selected at render time:

```scss
// styles/themes.scss
[data-tenant="afribus"] {
  --primary-color: #ff6600;
  --secondary-color: #333;
  --font-family: 'Roboto', sans-serif;
}

[data-tenant="other-tenant"] {
  --primary-color: #0066ff;
  --secondary-color: #eee;
  --font-family: 'Open Sans', sans-serif;
}
```

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
import './styles/themes.scss';

export default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const tenant = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_TENANT || 'default';
  return (
    <html lang="en" data-tenant={tenant}>
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

All your components use `var(--primary-color)` etc. No FOUC, works with Turbopack, ships minimal CSS.

## Option 2: React 19 `<link>` tag for runtime loading

Next.js App Router with React 19 supports `<link rel="stylesheet">` as a first-class component. You can conditionally load pre-compiled CSS at runtime:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const tenant = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_TENANT || 'default';
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <head>
        <link
          rel="stylesheet"
          href={`/styles/${tenant}-main.css`}
          precedence="default"
        />
      </head>
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

The catch: files must be pre-compiled `.css` in `public/styles/` (not `.scss`), so you'd need a build script to compile the Sass files first. The `precedence` prop is required for React 19's built-in stylesheet deduplication.

## Option 3: Conditional dynamic imports with hardcoded paths

You can't use variables in import paths, but you can use a switch with string literals — the bundler resolves each one statically and creates separate chunks:

```tsx
'use client';
import { useEffect } from 'react';

export default function TenantStyles() {
  useEffect(() => {
    const tenant = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_TENANT;
    switch (tenant) {
      case 'afribus':
        import('../styles/afribus-main.scss');
        break;
      case 'other':
        import('../styles/other-main.scss');
        break;
      default:
        import('../styles/default-main.scss');
    }
  }, []);
  return null;
}
```

Downside: client-side only, so there's a flash of unstyled content on first load.

## Option 4: Separate builds per tenant

If you deploy per-tenant anyway, use `next.config.js` to set Sass include paths based on the env var:

```js
const tenant = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_TENANT || 'default';
module.exports = {
  sassOptions: {
    includePaths: [`./styles/tenants/${tenant}`],
  },
};
```

Then your SCSS does `@use 'theme'` and it resolves to the tenant-specific directory. Most robust if you already have per-tenant CI/CD.

---

For most multi-tenant setups, **Option 1** (CSS custom properties) is the way to go — zero build complexity, no FOUC, works everywhere.

This is a fundamental constraint of how streaming works with `redirect()`. Once a Suspense boundary starts streaming, the HTTP headers (including the status code) are already committed as 200 - so `redirect()` can't go back and change them to a 30x. Instead it falls back to inserting a `<meta http-equiv="refresh">` tag into the stream.

Looking at the Next.js source, `redirect()` throws a `RedirectError`, and when that error is caught during streaming, [`make-get-server-inserted-html.tsx`](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/main/packages/next/src/server/app-render/make-get-server-inserted-html.tsx) handles it by injecting the meta tag. The [redirect() docs](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/redirect) note this too: "When used in a streaming context, this will insert a meta tag to emit the redirect on the client side."

With `cacheComponents: true`, your `searchParams` access forces the component into Suspense, which triggers streaming - so you'll always hit this path.

The workaround that should give you proper HTTP 30x redirects is to move the redirect logic into middleware (`proxy.ts` in Next.js 16):

```typescript
// proxy.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'
import type { NextRequest } from 'next/server'

export async function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
  if (request.nextUrl.pathname === '/jobs') {
    const filter = request.nextUrl.searchParams.get('filter')
    if (filter) {
      const res = await fetch(
        `https://api.example.com/jobs?filter=${filter}`,
        { redirect: 'manual' }
      )
      if (res.status === 301) {
        return NextResponse.redirect(
          res.headers.get('Location')!,
          { status: 301 }
        )
      }
    }
  }
  return NextResponse.next()
}

export const config = {
  matcher: '/jobs',
}
```

Middleware runs before rendering starts, so it can set HTTP headers properly. The trade-off is that you're moving backend logic out of the page component, but for redirect-only checks it's a clean fit.

Worth noting this is a known gap the team is tracking - Dan Abramov opened [#86739](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/86739) about the same pattern ("how to intentionally block in Cache Components"), and [#84196](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/84196) covers the `loading.tsx` variant of the same issue.

Let me know if the middleware approach works for your case.

Yeah, you've identified the race condition correctly - Auth.js acknowledges the same problem in their [refresh token rotation docs](https://authjs.dev/guides/refresh-token-rotation) with no built-in solution yet ([Discussion #3940](https://github.com/nextauthjs/next-auth/discussions/3940) has been open since 2022).

Your module-level `Map` approach was on the right track. The key is storing the **in-flight Promise itself**, not a flag or result - a flag creates a TOCTOU race where parallel requests slip between check and set. Sharing the Promise means all callers await the same fetch:

```typescript
// proxy.ts
const inflightRefreshes = new Map<string, Promise<Response>>();

async function refreshTokens(refreshToken: string): Promise<Response> {
  const existing = inflightRefreshes.get(refreshToken);
  if (existing) return existing; // all parallel requests share this Promise

  const promise = fetch(`${DJANGO_URL}/api/login/refresh/auto/`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ refresh: refreshToken }),
  }).finally(() => {
    inflightRefreshes.delete(refreshToken);
  });

  inflightRefreshes.set(refreshToken, promise);
  return promise;
}
```

In your `checkAuth`, replace the direct fetch with `await refreshTokens(refresh)`. All 6 parallel RSC requests hit the Map, but only the first one calls Django.

One caveat - the Next.js docs [warn against module-level state](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions/proxy) in proxy.ts because it can run on CDN/Edge where state doesn't persist. Since you're self-hosting with `npm run start`, module state persists in the Node.js process - safe for your setup, just wouldn't work on Vercel.

Your `BLACKLIST_AFTER_ROTATION=False` is reasonable for now. Longer term, you could also refresh proactively a couple of minutes before expiry in proxy.ts - that way navigations never hit the race window at all.

@nathanschram's in-flight Promise deduplication is the correct fix for the immediate race condition. I want to add two things:

**Why your Redis lock failed**

Your Django-side lock (`cache.add`) returns 429 for the "losing" requests, but your `proxy.ts` only handles 200 and treats everything else (including 429) as an auth failure → logout. If you retry the Redis approach, you'd need `proxy.ts` to retry on 429 after a short delay. But the client-side Promise deduplication is cleaner since it avoids the extra round-trip entirely.

**Proactive refresh eliminates the race window entirely**

Instead of waiting for the access token to expire and then racing to refresh, refresh it **before** it expires:

```typescript
function shouldRefreshProactively(access: string): boolean {
  const payload = JSON.parse(atob(access.split('.')[1]));
  const expiresIn = payload.exp * 1000 - Date.now();
  return expiresIn < 2 * 60 * 1000; // refresh 2 min before expiry
}

async function checkAuth(request: NextRequest) {
  const access = request.cookies.get('access')?.value;
  const refresh = request.cookies.get('refresh')?.value;

  if (access && !isTokenExpired(access) && !shouldRefreshProactively(access)) {
    return null; // valid and not near expiry
  }

  if (access && !isTokenExpired(access)) {
    // Token still valid but expiring soon — refresh in background
    // Even if this fails, the request proceeds with the current token
    refreshTokens(refresh).catch(() => {});
    return null;
  }

  // Token expired — must refresh before proceeding
  if (refresh) {
    const res = await refreshTokens(refresh); // deduped via Map
    // handle response...
  }
}
```

This way, the refresh happens during normal navigation while the access token is still valid. By the time it actually expires, you already have a new one. The race window effectively disappears.

**Combined approach:** Use the in-flight Promise deduplication (nathanschram's answer) + proactive refresh. This lets you keep `BLACKLIST_AFTER_ROTATION=True` safely.

To summarize the root cause and the best solution path:

## Root Cause

Next.js App Router fires **parallel RSC requests** on navigation (layout + page + nested segments). When the access token expires mid-navigation, all requests hit `proxy.ts` simultaneously — each sees an expired token and tries to refresh with the same `R1` refresh token. With `ROTATE_REFRESH_TOKENS=True` + `BLACKLIST_AFTER_ROTATION=True`, only the first refresh succeeds; the rest get 401 (blacklisted token) → logout.

## Recommended Solution: In-Flight Promise Deduplication + Proactive Refresh

Combine both approaches for a robust fix:

### 1. In-Flight Promise Deduplication (fixes the race)

```typescript
// proxy.ts
const inflightRefreshes = new Map<string, Promise<Response>>();

async function refreshTokens(refreshToken: string): Promise<Response> {
  const existing = inflightRefreshes.get(refreshToken);
  if (existing) return existing; // all parallel requests share the same Promise

  const promise = fetch(`${DJANGO_URL}/api/login/refresh/auto/`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ refresh: refreshToken }),
  }).finally(() => {
    inflightRefreshes.delete(refreshToken);
  });

  inflightRefreshes.set(refreshToken, promise);
  return promise;
}
```

Storing the **Promise itself** (not a flag) means all 6 parallel RSC requests await the **same** Django call — only one refresh hits your backend.

> **Self-hosting note:** Module-level state persists in a Node.js process (`npm run start`), so this works reliably on your VPS setup. It would not work on Vercel Edge/CDN where each invocation is stateless.

### 2. Proactive Refresh (eliminates the race window entirely)

```typescript
function shouldRefreshProactively(access: string): boolean {
  const payload = JSON.parse(atob(access.split('.')[1]));
  const expiresIn = payload.exp * 1000 - Date.now();
  return expiresIn < 2 * 60 * 1000; // refresh 2 min before expiry
}
```

Trigger a background refresh when the token is still valid but expiring soon. By the time users navigate at the expiry boundary, the new token is already in place — no race to trigger.

## Decision Matrix

| Approach | Fixes Race | Keeps Blacklisting | Complexity |
|---|---|---|---|
| `BLACKLIST_AFTER_ROTATION=False` | ✅ | ❌ | Low |
| In-flight Promise dedup | ✅ | ✅ | Medium |
| Proactive refresh only | Mostly ✅ | ✅ | Medium |
| Both combined | ✅✅ | ✅ | Medium |

The combined approach (in-flight dedup + proactive refresh) lets you keep `BLACKLIST_AFTER_ROTATION=True` without the security tradeoff.

