Yeah that's actually intentional - [PR #82114](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/pull/82114) explicitly removed header forwarding from the image optimizer's internal fetch as a security fix ([CVE-2025-57752](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-g5qg-72qw-gw5v)). The problem was that the cache key doesn't include headers, so user A's authenticated image could get cached and served to user B.

Since your images are semi-public though, this is actually fine - there's no per-user content to leak. The simplest fix is to exclude your proxy route from auth middleware (or just not check cookies in the proxy handler). The proxy is server-internal anyway - a request reaching it means the page already passed auth. The path-based URLs aren't guessable from outside.

If you do want auth on the proxy route itself, the other option is exactly what you said - build optimisation into the proxy with `sharp` and use a [custom loader](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/image#loaderfile) to bypass the built-in optimizer entirely:

```typescript
// app/api/image/route.ts
import sharp from 'sharp'

export async function GET(req: NextRequest) {
  // Full cookie/header access here
  const session = await getSession(req)
  if (!session) return new NextResponse('Unauthorized', { status: 401 })

  const path = req.nextUrl.searchParams.get('path')!
  const width = parseInt(req.nextUrl.searchParams.get('w') || '0')
  const quality = parseInt(req.nextUrl.searchParams.get('q') || '75')

  const signedUrl = await generateSignedUrl(path)
  const upstream = await fetch(signedUrl)
  const buffer = Buffer.from(await upstream.arrayBuffer())

  const optimised = await sharp(buffer)
    .resize(width || undefined)
    .webp({ quality })
    .toBuffer()

  return new NextResponse(optimised, {
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'image/webp',
      'Cache-Control': 'public, max-age=31536000, immutable',
    },
  })
}
```

This gives you cookies, real WebP/AVIF conversion, and you control the cache headers. `sharp` is what Next.js uses internally anyway so it's well-supported.

