+1 to @icyJoseph's instinct — this is almost always client-side script accretion across SPA navigations rather than the server-rendered HTML actually shipping the JSON-LD twice. Two diagnostic steps that pin it down quickly:

**1. Look at the raw HTML, not the validator.**

```bash
curl -s https://your-site.example/the-page | grep -c 'application/ld+json'
```

If that returns `1`, the server-rendered output is correct and the duplicate is being introduced after hydration — a `<script>` tag appended in `useEffect` (or by a hook from a SEO library) that doesn't clean itself up on unmount/route-change is the usual culprit.

If it returns `2`, the source is your component tree — either a layout and a page both render the same JSON-LD block, or you've combined Next's `metadata` export with a manually-rendered `<script type="application/ld+json">` and got both.

**2. The reliable fix in Next.js 13+ App Router is the metadata API** — it dedupes by default and gets cleaned up by the framework on navigation:

```tsx
// app/products/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateMetadata({ params }): Promise<Metadata> {
  const product = await getProduct(params.slug);
  return {
    other: {
      'application/ld+json': JSON.stringify({
        '@context': 'https://schema.org',
        '@type': 'Product',
        name: product.name,
        // ...
      }),
    },
  };
}
```

Or, if you need to keep imperative control (e.g. dynamic schema after a client fetch), wrap the inject in a cleanup-aware effect:

```tsx
'use client';
import { useEffect } from 'react';

export function StructuredData({ schema }: { schema: object }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    const tag = document.createElement('script');
    tag.type = 'application/ld+json';
    tag.dataset.seoManaged = '1';
    tag.textContent = JSON.stringify(schema);
    document.head.appendChild(tag);
    return () => {
      // critical — without this, every SPA navigation leaves a fresh script behind
      tag.remove();
    };
  }, [schema]);
  return null;
}
```

The `data-seo-managed="1"` marker is also handy for a one-liner sweep at app boot if you suspect older builds left orphans:

```ts
document.head.querySelectorAll('script[type="application/ld+json"]:not([data-seo-managed])').forEach(s => s.remove());
```

If your `curl` count is 1 and the validator still complains, paste the validator's raw output — Google's Rich Results test, in particular, sometimes follows redirects + `next-prefetch` resources and double-counts in a confusing way that's not actually duplicated in the served document.

Let's walk through this together. In a client-driven app, an Axios interceptor lets every **request** repair itself: catch a 401, call `/refresh-token`, retry. The interceptor lives at the request layer, so your component tree never knows it happened. 

With the App Router, server-rendered work has access to cookies and headers but runs before any client JavaScript. There's no interceptor; there's a request context. 

The mental shift is from "any request can repair itself" to "any **render** can repair itself." Same idea, different layer. Let's see what that looks like in two places: a read path and a write path.

**Read.** A Server Component forwards the browser's cookies to your upstream:

```ts
import { cookies } from "next/headers";

export async function getCart() {
  const jar = await cookies();
  const res = await fetch(`${API_URL}/cart`, {
    headers: { Cookie: jar.toString() },
    cache: "no-store",
  });
  const body = await res.json();
  return res.ok
    ? { ok: true, data: body.data }
    : { ok: false, reason: body.reason };
}
```

**Write.** Server Components can read cookies but not write them. When Express returns `Set-Cookie` on `/refresh-token`, your Next.js server received it; the browser did not. To get the new tokens onto the user's response, parse `Set-Cookie` in a Server Action (or Route Handler) and call `cookieStore.set(...)`.

The page branches on the read result:

```tsx
export default async function CartPage() {
  const result = await getCart();

  if (result.ok) return <Cart data={result.data} />;

  if (result.reason === "access_token_expired") return <RefreshSessionAndRetry />;

  return <LoginRequired />;
}
```

The Server Action does the actual repair: forward the user's cookies upstream, parse `Set-Cookie` from the response, write the new pair via `cookieStore.set`, then `revalidatePath` so the action's response carries a fresh RSC payload.

```ts
"use server";

import { cookies } from "next/headers";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";
import { revalidatePath } from "next/cache";

// call this server function in a useEffect within RefreshSessionAndRetry
export async function refreshSession() {
  const jar = await cookies();

  const res = await fetch(`${API_URL}/refresh-token`, {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { Cookie: jar.toString() },
    cache: "no-store",
  });

  if (res.status === 401) {
    jar.delete("access_token");
    jar.delete("refresh_token");
    redirect("/login");
  }

  // Replay upstream Set-Cookie headers onto the user's response, otherwise
  // the browser never stores the new tokens.
  forwardSetCookies(res, jar);

  revalidatePath("/cart");
}
```

`forwardSetCookies` is a small helper you'll need to write. Each entry from `res.headers.getSetCookie()` is a single `Set-Cookie` string (`name=value; HttpOnly; Path=/; Max-Age=300; SameSite=Lax`); split the name/value off the front, map the attributes onto the options object that `cookieStore.set` accepts, and call `jar.set(name, value, options)` per entry. The MDN [`Set-Cookie` syntax page](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Set-Cookie) is the authoritative grammar, or pull in one of the small `set-cookie` parsers on npm if you'd rather not roll it yourself.

`<RefreshSessionAndRetry>` is a `"use client"` component that fires a Server Action from `useEffect`. The action calls `/refresh-token`, forwards `Set-Cookie` via `cookieStore.set`, and ends with `revalidatePath("/cart")` (or `redirect` on 401). The page re-renders; the read succeeds; `<Cart>` shows.

```text
First render:    expired access  → render boundary
Client:          fires Server Action
Server Action:   refresh, write cookies, revalidate
Second render:   read succeeds   → render Cart
```

> With a fast network, the user doesn't even see the `refreshing session` UI - notice at the end, a new access-token and refresh-token

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/27fd54a5-b457-4cfe-8194-c82c2799fee8

> With slow network 

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b6b4fe9e-8a3f-4f52-9090-3f4ee1bd34e1

Where this differs from Axios, and how to close the gap: the interceptor never touches your component tree; it sits at the request layer. The render boundary, taken literally, unmounts the protected subtree on the render where the read failed (render N: `<Cart>`, render N+1: boundary, render N+2: `<Cart>`). Two structural moves make that cost go away:

- **Stateful UI in a wrapper that always renders.** Structure the page as `<CartShell>{result.ok ? <Items /> : <Boundary />}</CartShell>`. `<CartShell>` is a client component holding interactive state; its position in the tree is stable, only its child swaps. Coupon inputs, quantity drafts, expanded panels survive the swap.
- **Mutations retry inside their own Server Action.** A 401-then-refresh-then-retry handled inside the action never lets revalidation hit a stale token, so the page never re-renders into the boundary mid-session. The boundary's job shrinks to cold entry, where there's no client state to preserve anyway.

Render the boundary's UI on a delay (~200ms) so fast refreshes don't flash a "refreshing" message at all. With the two moves plus the delay, the perceived behavior matches the interceptor: state preserved, fast cases instantaneous, only slow cold-entry refreshes surface explanation. Axios avoids unmount because every request retries; the boundary avoids it because the stateful frame doesn't move and the operations that would trigger a re-render are themselves auth-aware.

About round trips: the boundary uses two client→Next trips (initial render returns the boundary, then a Server Action refreshes and revalidates) where the Axios recovery is three client→Express trips (fail, refresh, retry). 

The boundary's Express calls happen on the fast Next→Express server-to-server link, not on the user's connection, so total perceived time is usually shorter even with the extra "trip." That trip is the cost of streaming the page shell first instead of blocking on refresh. 

One more gap worth naming: an Axios interceptor is declared once and every request inherits it; our pattern makes every protected page write its own three-way branch. To get back to "declare once," group your protected pages under a route group like `app/(protected)/...` and drop a single `error.tsx` at that level. Read helpers throw on `access_token_expired`; the boundary catches, runs `refreshSession`, and lets `revalidatePath` (or `reset()`) push the new tree. Pages just `await getCart()` and use the data. 

If you want to collapse it back to a single client trip while still persisting refreshed cookies, lift the refresh into `proxy.js`: it runs before the page renders, calls `/refresh-token` when the access cookie is stale, and writes new cookies on the response, and upstream going request. Trade-off: `proxy.js` runs on every matched request, so prefer a local JWT signature/expiry check over a remote call when you can.

Sharp edges worth knowing about:

- **Concurrent refreshes (multi-tab).** Strict rotation will 401 the second tab. Use rotation with a short grace window: idempotent re-issue inside, family revocation on replay outside.
- **Render loops.** Cap consecutive auto-refresh attempts with a counter cookie; past the cap, clear cookies and redirect to `/login`.
- **Network vs. session errors.** 401 means session gone (clear, redirect). Network error means session probably fine (throw, surface retry UI; do not log the user out).

Net result: client code is unchanged for browser-driven flows; server-rendered work uses cookies plus a server-side refresh that does what the interceptor was doing, just in the request-context layer where Server Components live.

