This happens because TypeScript does not always preserve discriminated union narrowing perfectly when generics are involved.

Your union itself is correct, but the condition:

```ts id="u7f4pc"
if (response.success)
```

does not narrow as strongly as:

```ts id="gj2n2v"
if (response.success === true)
```

In generic contexts, TypeScript can sometimes widen boolean checks and lose the discriminator information.

This version works reliably:

```ts id="9r0jlwm"
type Success<T> = {
  success: true
  data: T
}

type Failure = {
  success: false
  error: string
}

type ApiResponse<T> = Success<T> | Failure

function handleResponse<T>(response: ApiResponse<T>) {
  if (response.success === true) {
    return response.data
  }

  return response.error
}
```

Another clean approach is using a custom type predicate:

```ts id="0v2f7y"
function isSuccess<T>(
  response: ApiResponse<T>
): response is Success<T> {
  return response.success
}
```

then:

```ts id="o4t4mb"
if (isSuccess(response)) {
  return response.data
}

return response.error
```

The type predicate approach scales better in larger codebases because it keeps narrowing logic reusable and explicit.

This behavior is related to TypeScript’s control flow analysis around generics and boolean discriminators, not an issue with your union structure itself.

