Your approach with useSearchParams is overcomplicating it. Here's the correct pattern:

Do this ,server-to-server, no client components needed

```tsx
// app/page.tsx
export default async function MyPage({
  searchParams,
}: {
  searchParams: Promise<{ id: string }>
}) {
  const { id } = await searchParams

  return (
    <>
      {/* This component doesn't need the ID — no prop passed */}
      <MyComponentThatDoesNotNeedTheId />

      {/* Pass ID directly to the component that needs it — no middleman */}
      <MyComplexTable id={id} />
    </>
  )
}

// app/MyComplexTable.tsx (Server Component)
export const MyComplexTable = ({ id }: { id: string }) => {
  // Fetch data using id here
  const data = await fetchData(id)
  return <Table data={data} />
}
```

What you tried, and why it fails

You tried to pass a server component (<MyTable id={id} />) as a prop to a client component (MyComplexTable). This doesn't work because server components can't be imported into client components — they must be passed as children using slots or composition.

The rule

Scenario Pattern
You need searchParams in a server component Pass it directly as a prop from page.tsx
You need searchParams in a client component Use useSearchParams() inside that client component
You need both (server + client) Read in page.tsx, pass to server component. Client component uses useSearchParams separately

Bottom line: Don't pass server components as props to client components. Use children or render the server component directly in page.tsx and pass data

