This happens because the text rendered during SSR has to match the text React produces on the first browser render. `new Date().toLocaleDateString()` is not stable for that: it depends on the current time, locale, and time zone. The Next.js hydration-error docs list time-dependent APIs like `Date()` in render as a common cause.

Also, for a registration date you probably do not want `new Date()` there; use the actual stored registration timestamp and format that.

For a user-local display, keep the page as a Server Component and move only the formatting into a small Client Component:

```tsx
// app/local-registration-date.tsx
'use client'

import { startTransition, useEffect, useState } from 'react'

export function LocalRegistrationDate({ date }: { date: string }) {
  const [label, setLabel] = useState<string | null>(null)

  useEffect(() => {
    startTransition(() => {
      setLabel(
        new Date(date).toLocaleDateString(undefined, {
          year: 'numeric',
          month: 'long',
          day: 'numeric',
        })
      )
    })
  }, [date])

  return (
    <time dateTime={date} suppressHydrationWarning>
      {label ?? '...'}
    </time>
  )
}
```

Then in the page:

```tsx
import { LocalRegistrationDate } from './local-registration-date'

export default async function Page() {
  const registeredAt = '2026-06-21T10:00:00Z' // replace with user.createdAt

  return (
    <div>
      Registered on: <LocalRegistrationDate date={registeredAt} />
    </div>
  )
}
```

The important part is that the initial render is deterministic (`...` on both server and first client render), and the locale-specific date is filled in after hydration. If you need no visible placeholder/flash at all, Next also has a “preventing flash before hydration” guide using an inline script plus `suppressHydrationWarning`, but for a dashboard label this small client component is usually the simpler fix.

hi! this hydration error happens because the server renders the time using the server's time zone (e.g., UTC) during SSR, but when the browser receives the HTML, it tries to re-render using your local machine's time zone. Since the strings don't match, React throws a hydration mismatch error.

here are the two best ways to solve this in Next.js depending on your needs:

my solution is to use `useEffect` to ensure client-only rendering (recommended)
You can also delay showing the date until the component has fully mounted on the client side.

```tsx
'use client';
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export default function Page() {
  const [mounted, setMounted] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setMounted(true);
  }, []);

  const formattedDate = new Date().toLocaleDateString();

  return (
    <div>
      Registered on: {mounted ? formattedDate : 'Loading...'}
    </div>
  );
}

