Yes — this split is exactly the shape I had in mind, and the way you've drawn the boundary between `di-craft/next/server + React.cache()` and `di-craft/node + AsyncLocalStorage` is cleaner than how I usually explain it. Two small refinements you might want to bake into the docs while you're there:

**1. `unstable_after` re-entry pattern.** In Next 15+, if a Server Action queues work with `unstable_after`, that callback runs after the ALS scope has already settled — so `getRequestContainer()` inside it will throw. The idiomatic fix is to *capture the primitives* the callback needs before scheduling, then re-enter a fresh minimal scope for the deferred work:

```ts
"use server";
export async function publishPost(input: FormData) {
  return withRequestContainer(async () => {
    const { db, tenantId } = getRequestContainer();
    const post = await db.insert(posts).values(fromForm(input)).returning();

    // capture what the deferred job needs — don't rely on ALS post-await
    const capturedTenantId = tenantId;
    const capturedPostId = post[0].id;

    unstable_after(async () => {
      await withRequestContainer(async () => {
        // fresh scope; ALS re-populated for this callback
        const { search } = getRequestContainer();
        await search.index(capturedTenantId, capturedPostId);
      }, { tenantId: capturedTenantId });
    });

    revalidateTag(`t:${tenantId}:posts`);
  });
}
```

Worth calling out in the "detached/post-action work" section — this is the concrete API shape most people will trip over.

**2. Edge runtime caveat.** `di-craft/node + AsyncLocalStorage` cleanly implies Node runtime. Worth an explicit note that on the Edge runtime (middleware, `export const runtime = "edge"` Route Handlers), `node:async_hooks` is polyfilled but the semantics are subtly different — the polyfill can lose context across some awaits in edge workers. Recommend readers stick with the Node runtime for the ALS path, or use an equivalent Edge-native context primitive if they need Edge. Otherwise people will copy the pattern verbatim, deploy with `runtime = "edge"`, and hit intermittent "container missing" errors that are hard to diagnose.

**3. Testing footnote.** For the vitest side of things, `withRequestContainer` composes nicely with a per-test container override — `beforeEach(() => runInContainer(makeTestContainer(), () => ...))` works out of the box because `AsyncLocalStorage` handles the nesting for you. Might be worth a one-liner in the README so people don't reach for `vi.mock`-based DI which is where things get painful.

The three-layer separation you settled on (render-scope / request-scope / client boundary) is the shape that actually holds up under production load. Nice work on the follow-up PR.

